### What are the three depth-first traversals?

All three visit every node exactly once by going as deep as possible before backtracking. They differ only in when the node itself is visited relative to its two subtrees. Preorder visits the node first, inorder visits it between the subtrees, and postorder visits it last.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class RecursiveTraversals {
    static class TreeNode {
        int val; TreeNode left, right;
        TreeNode(int v) { val = v; }
    }

    static void preorder(TreeNode node, List<Integer> res) {
        if (node == null) return;
        res.add(node.val);         // visit
        preorder(node.left, res);  // left
        preorder(node.right, res); // right
    }

    static void inorder(TreeNode node, List<Integer> res) {
        if (node == null) return;
        inorder(node.left, res);   // left
        res.add(node.val);         // visit
        inorder(node.right, res);  // right
    }

    static void postorder(TreeNode node, List<Integer> res) {
        if (node == null) return;
        postorder(node.left, res);  // left
        postorder(node.right, res); // right
        res.add(node.val);          // visit
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        TreeNode root = new TreeNode(1);
        root.left = new TreeNode(2);
        root.right = new TreeNode(3);
        root.left.left = new TreeNode(4);
        root.left.right = new TreeNode(5);

        List<Integer> pre = new ArrayList<>(), in = new ArrayList<>(), post = new ArrayList<>();
        preorder(root, pre); inorder(root, in); postorder(root, post);
        System.out.println("Preorder:  " + pre);
        System.out.println("Inorder:   " + in);
        System.out.println("Postorder: " + post);
    }
}
```

```text
Preorder:  [1, 2, 4, 5, 3]
Inorder:   [4, 2, 5, 1, 3]
Postorder: [4, 5, 2, 3, 1]
```

### Why do we need iterative versions?

Recursion uses the JVM call stack implicitly. On a very tall tree, deep recursion can cause `StackOverflowError`. The iterative versions use an explicit `ArrayDeque` stack allocated on the heap, which can grow as large as available memory. This makes them safe for production use on trees of any height.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class WhyIterative {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Recursive: uses JVM call stack (default ~512KB-1MB)");
        System.out.println("Tall tree (100K nodes deep): StackOverflowError");
        System.out.println("Iterative: uses heap-allocated ArrayDeque");
        System.out.println("Can handle trees of any height");
    }
}
```

```text
Recursive: uses JVM call stack (default ~512KB-1MB)
Tall tree (100K nodes deep): StackOverflowError
Iterative: uses heap-allocated ArrayDeque
Can handle trees of any height
```

### How does iterative preorder work?

Push the root onto the stack. Pop a node, visit it, then push its right child before its left child. Because the stack is LIFO, the left child will be processed first, which gives the correct node-left-right order.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Deque;
import java.util.List;

public class IterativePreorder {
    static class TreeNode {
        int val; TreeNode left, right;
        TreeNode(int v) { val = v; }
    }

    public static List<Integer> preorder(TreeNode root) {
        List<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>();
        if (root == null) return result;

        Deque<TreeNode> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
        stack.push(root);

        while (!stack.isEmpty()) {
            TreeNode node = stack.pop();
            result.add(node.val);
            if (node.right != null) stack.push(node.right); // right first
            if (node.left != null) stack.push(node.left);   // left on top
        }
        return result;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        TreeNode root = new TreeNode(1);
        root.left = new TreeNode(2);
        root.right = new TreeNode(3);
        root.left.left = new TreeNode(4);
        root.left.right = new TreeNode(5);
        System.out.println("Iterative preorder: " + preorder(root));
    }
}
```

```text
Iterative preorder: [1, 2, 4, 5, 3]
```

### How does iterative inorder work?

Keep a current pointer and a stack. Push nodes as you go left. When you cannot go further left, pop a node, visit it, then move to its right child. This simulates the left-node-right order exactly.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Deque;
import java.util.List;

public class IterativeInorder {
    static class TreeNode {
        int val; TreeNode left, right;
        TreeNode(int v) { val = v; }
    }

    public static List<Integer> inorder(TreeNode root) {
        List<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>();
        Deque<TreeNode> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
        TreeNode curr = root;

        while (curr != null || !stack.isEmpty()) {
            while (curr != null) {
                stack.push(curr);
                curr = curr.left;
            }
            curr = stack.pop();
            result.add(curr.val);
            curr = curr.right;
        }
        return result;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        TreeNode root = new TreeNode(1);
        root.left = new TreeNode(2);
        root.right = new TreeNode(3);
        root.left.left = new TreeNode(4);
        root.left.right = new TreeNode(5);
        System.out.println("Iterative inorder: " + inorder(root));
    }
}
```

```text
Iterative inorder: [4, 2, 5, 1, 3]
```

### How does iterative postorder work?

The cleanest trick is to do a modified preorder that visits node-right-left, then reverse the result to get left-right-node (postorder). The modified preorder pushes left before right instead of right before left.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.Deque;
import java.util.List;

public class IterativePostorder {
    static class TreeNode {
        int val; TreeNode left, right;
        TreeNode(int v) { val = v; }
    }

    public static List<Integer> postorder(TreeNode root) {
        List<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>();
        if (root == null) return result;

        Deque<TreeNode> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
        stack.push(root);

        while (!stack.isEmpty()) {
            TreeNode node = stack.pop();
            result.add(node.val);
            if (node.left != null) stack.push(node.left);   // left first
            if (node.right != null) stack.push(node.right); // right on top
        }
        Collections.reverse(result); // node-right-left reversed = left-right-node
        return result;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        TreeNode root = new TreeNode(1);
        root.left = new TreeNode(2);
        root.right = new TreeNode(3);
        root.left.left = new TreeNode(4);
        root.left.right = new TreeNode(5);
        System.out.println("Iterative postorder: " + postorder(root));
    }
}
```

```text
Iterative postorder: [4, 5, 2, 3, 1]
```

### Why is inorder special for BSTs?

Inorder traversal of a binary search tree produces the values in sorted ascending order. This property makes inorder the natural choice for BST validation, kth-smallest-element, and range queries. If the inorder output is not sorted, the tree is not a valid BST.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Deque;
import java.util.List;

public class BstInorderSorted {
    static class TreeNode {
        int val; TreeNode left, right;
        TreeNode(int v) { val = v; }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // BST:     5
        //        /   \
        //       3     7
        //      / \     \
        //     2   4     8
        TreeNode root = new TreeNode(5);
        root.left = new TreeNode(3);
        root.right = new TreeNode(7);
        root.left.left = new TreeNode(2);
        root.left.right = new TreeNode(4);
        root.right.right = new TreeNode(8);

        List<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>();
        Deque<TreeNode> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
        TreeNode curr = root;
        while (curr != null || !stack.isEmpty()) {
            while (curr != null) { stack.push(curr); curr = curr.left; }
            curr = stack.pop();
            result.add(curr.val);
            curr = curr.right;
        }
        System.out.println("BST inorder (sorted): " + result);
    }
}
```

```text
BST inorder (sorted): [2, 3, 4, 5, 7, 8]
```

### What about time and space complexity?

All six implementations (3 recursive, 3 iterative) are O(n) time because each node is visited exactly once. Space is O(h) where h is the height of the tree, for the stack (implicit or explicit). For a balanced tree h is log n; for a skewed tree h is n.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class TraversalComplexity {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Time: O(n) for all six versions");
        System.out.println("Space: O(h) where h = tree height");
        System.out.println("  Balanced: h = log n");
        System.out.println("  Skewed:   h = n");
    }
}
```

```text
Time: O(n) for all six versions
Space: O(h) where h = tree height
  Balanced: h = log n
  Skewed:   h = n
```

### How is this applied in banking?

At the National Bank of India, I used these traversals on a fee expression tree. Preorder gave a prefix export for a downstream system. Inorder reconstructed the human-readable infix formula for display. Postorder produced exactly the token order that a stack-based evaluator consumes. The iterative versions were kept alongside the recursive ones for production safety on deep expression trees.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class FeeExpressionTree {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Expression tree for: (base + tax) * rate
        //         *
        //        / \
        //       +   rate
        //      / \
        //   base  tax
        System.out.println("Preorder (prefix):   * + base tax rate");
        System.out.println("Inorder (infix):     base + tax * rate");
        System.out.println("Postorder (postfix): base tax + rate *");
        System.out.println("Postfix feeds directly into the stack evaluator");
    }
}
```

```text
Preorder (prefix):   * + base tax rate
Inorder (infix):     base + tax * rate
Postorder (postfix): base tax + rate *
Postfix feeds directly into the stack evaluator
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Morris traversal achieves O(1) space by temporarily threading the tree using right pointers, visiting each node without a stack or recursion. It is rarely used in production because it mutates the tree temporarily, but it demonstrates deep understanding of tree structure. Another senior point is that iterative postorder without the reverse trick requires tracking whether the right subtree has been visited, which is more complex but avoids the O(n) reversal.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class SeniorTraversalNotes {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Morris traversal: O(1) space, threads tree temporarily");
        System.out.println("True iterative postorder: uses prev-pointer, no reverse");
        System.out.println("Inorder on BST = sorted order (key property)");
        System.out.println("All six versions: O(n) time, O(h) space");
    }
}
```

```text
Morris traversal: O(1) space, threads tree temporarily
True iterative postorder: uses prev-pointer, no reverse
Inorder on BST = sorted order (key property)
All six versions: O(n) time, O(h) space
```
